{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`fsck` is a DIAGNOSTIC tool: finding problems is its purpose and its success condition. The MCP\ntool nevertheless converts a completed report into `command_failed`, so the caller receives an\nerror object with the entire report stuffed into a message STRING rather than a structured\nresult.\n\n## Mechanism (runtime-confirmed — see the RCA comment)\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py:242-243` — the CLI exits non-zero when `issue_count > 0`.\n  **Correct; NOT the defect.**\n- `src/rebar/_lib_reads.py:347-353` — calls `fsck_cli([])` (TEXT mode, so it never asks for the\n  JSON the CLI can already produce) and raises `RebarError(f\"rebar fsck failed (exit {rc}): ...\")`\n  on ANY non-zero rc, flattening the report into an exception message. **This is the defect.**\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:385,396` — `def fsck(...) -> str` (so no `outputSchema`) returning\n  `rebar.fsck(...)`, so that raise reaches the client as\n  `{\"error\":\"command_failed\",\"exit_code\":1,\"message\":\"<whole report>\"}`.\n\nVerified: on a store with findings, `rebar fsck --output json` returns rc 1 AND a structured\n`{\"issues\":[{\"kind\",\"ticket_id\",\"detail\"}],\"fixed\":[],\"issue_count\":2}` — while `rebar.fsck()`\nraises on the same store. The structure exists one layer down and is discarded.\n\n## What is EXPECTED and what is the BUG\n\n**Expected: the CLI exiting non-zero on findings.** Conventional for an integrity checker\n(`git fsck`, `fsck(8)`); scripts depend on it. Not a defect. **The bug is the library/MCP layer\ntreating that as a FAILED CALL.**\n\n## Two commands, two contracts (established from source; corrected after code review)\n\n`fsck` and `fsck-recover` are DIFFERENT operations and must not share a reader:\n\n| | `fsck` (scan) | `fsck-recover` |\n|---|---|---|\n| output | tagged `KIND:` lines; has `--output json` | plain-English NARRATIVE; no JSON mode |\n| exit 0 | clean | nothing to do / recovered |\n| exit 1 | issues found (completed) | attempted but nothing recovered (completed) |\n| exit 2 | usage / uninitialised store | fatal (no tracker / bad args) |\n\nSources: `_commands/fsck.py:17-18,242-243`; `_commands/fsck_recover.py:9-11,200-320`.\nAn uninitialised store exits **1**, not 2 (`_missing_tracker_result`, `fsck.py:246-268`), and in\nJSON mode emits a payload byte-identical to a clean store.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **`src/rebar/_lib_reads.py` — new `fsck_report(*, recover=False, report_only=False,\n   repo_root=None) -> dict`**, a thin dispatcher over two private readers with SEPARATE\n   exit-code contracts:\n   - `_fsck_scan_report` — passes `--output=json` so the canonical shape comes from the CLI's own\n     transform (text and JSON cannot drift). Returns\n     `{issues, fixed, issue_count, returncode, mode: \"scan\", report: None}` for a completed run\n     (exit 0 or 1). Raises on exit outside `(0, 1)`, and — SCAN ONLY — on exit 1 with an empty\n     `issues[]`, which is unreachable for a real scan (the exit derives from the COUNTED tally\n     while `issues[]` is the superset of every emitted `KIND:` line) and therefore identifies the\n     uninitialised/unscannable store. Without it the function would report a store it never read\n     as clean.\n   - `_fsck_recover_report` — captures the narrative VERBATIM into `report` and never runs it\n     through the scan's line transform (which would scrape `{\"kind\": \"warn\"}` entries out of the\n     narrative's `WARN:` prose and discard the rest). Returns\n     `{mode: \"recover\", report, issues: [], fixed: [], issue_count: 0, returncode}` for exit 0 or\n     1; raises on exit 2. It must NOT inherit the scan's exit-1 rule — recover's exit 1 is a\n     completed recovery.\n   - `mode` is the discriminator, so a recover result can never be misread as a clean scan.\n2. **`rebar.fsck()` is left exactly as it is** — `-> str`, raising on non-zero. It has ~10\n   existing callers across `tests/interfaces/**` that assert on its string return.\n3. **`src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` — add `FsckOut`** mirroring `src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json`\n   (which ALREADY EXISTS, registered as `schemas.FSCK`), following the `_Out` /\n   `ClarityResultOut` / `BridgeFsckOut` pattern, incl. `mode` and `report`.\n4. **`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — `fsck` returns `FsckOut`** built from `rebar.fsck_report()`, so\n   the tool advertises an `outputSchema` like every read tool registered around it. `recover` and\n   `report_only`/`REBAR_MCP_READONLY` handling is unchanged. One tool, one schema, two modes.\n5. **`src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json`** — declare `returncode`, `mode` and `report` as\n   OPTIONAL (kept out of `required` so the CLI's own `--output json` payload still validates\n   unchanged), and register `FsckOut -> fsck` in `check_mcp_models.MODEL_SCHEMAS`.\n6. **AC4 (`src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py`)** — `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` stops incrementing the\n   issue counter; the line and its `issues[]` entry are unchanged. Reasoning in the AC4 comment.\n7. **Docs**: `docs/user-guide.md` (never-counted class, beside the `TRACKER_DIRTY_TMP_EVENT`\n   precedent), `docs/concurrency.md` I8, and `docs/output-schemas.md` (the MCP `fsck` outputSchema\n   exemption is no longer true).\n\n## Test plan\n\nRED first, held out from the implementer:\n`tests/interfaces/facades/test_fsck_structured_326f.py` asserts the MCP-layer call returns an\nenumerable structured result rather than raising; that a run which could not HAPPEN still raises\nand is distinguishable; that `rebar.fsck()` and the CLI exit are unchanged; that\n`STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` is emitted but not counted; and — covering the paths whose absence let a\nblocker through review — that the RECOVER path preserves its narrative, invents no findings from\nits `WARN:` prose, treats exit 1 as a completed recovery, still raises on exit 2, and that the\nscan's `rc not in (0, 1)` branch raises. Mutation-checked by re-seeding each confirmed defect\n(including routing recover back through the transform, and applying the scan's exit-1 rule to\nrecover) and confirming RED.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The MCP `fsck` tool returns a STRUCTURED result (findings enumerable by kind and ticket)\n      for a completed run, whatever the findings, and advertises an `outputSchema`.\n- [x] A genuine execution failure (fsck could not run) remains distinguishable from a completed\n      run that found issues, on BOTH the scan and recover paths, whose exit-code vocabularies\n      differ.\n- [x] The recover path is represented as the distinct operation it is: its narrative is preserved\n      verbatim and never parsed as a scan report.\n- [x] The CLI's non-zero-on-findings exit is PRESERVED — scripts depend on it; only the\n      library/MCP surfacing changes.\n- [x] `rebar.fsck()`'s existing `-> str`/raise contract is unchanged for its existing callers.\n- [x] Decided and recorded whether self-resolved `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` entries count toward the\n      failure exit; the CLI's exit reflects that decision.\n\n## Reported by\n\nA parallel Copilot CLI session against the deployed server.\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `fsck` called through the deployed endpoint returns a STRUCTURED result with enumerable findings, not `command_failed`.\n      Evidence must be OBSERVED against the deployed server at\n      `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` and recorded in a ticket comment naming\n      the tool(s) called, the arguments, and the actual response. A passing local test is\n      NOT sufficient, and neither is a merged change: the container must have redeployed\n      and served the call. This exists because a fix here was previously reported complete\n      on the strength of a merge while the deployed behaviour was still broken."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787875495995341052,"uuid":"78603db6-cdf2-46fa-9669-2b184e0fc905"}